        "Pretending to know may preserve an image briefly while delaying competence. Admitting uncertainty gives learning a starting point. Humility means accurately recognising what you know and what still needs work.\n\nIn practice, this idea is less about achieving a perfect outcome than improving the quality of the process. People make wiser choices when they can explain what they know, recognise what remains uncertain, and review the result honestly. That approach leaves room for mistakes and correction without treating every setback as proof that the whole effort was pointless.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “Learn Without Performing Expertise” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
        "Ask one genuine clarifying question this week. Note how the answer changed your understanding or next action.\n\nMake the practice concrete rather than leaving it as a good intention. Decide when you will act, what might interrupt you, and what you will do if the first attempt does not work. A short note or calendar reminder can help. The goal is not to control every circumstance; it is to make your response more deliberate and easier to repeat.\n\nFinal reminder for this chapter: do not wait for perfect confidence before taking a reasonable step. Start carefully, learn from the result, and adjust where needed."
