        "Presence Is More Than Proximity",
        "Two neighbours lived close but rarely spoke beyond greetings. Another friend lived far away and remembered important dates, asked thoughtful questions, and made time for honest conversations.\n\nA real-life scenario: Imagine two friends have agreed to help each other prepare for an important event. One has quietly taken on most of the work and begins to feel used; the other assumes everything is fine because no complaint has been made. When the first friend explains the imbalance calmly and asks to divide the remaining tasks, both have a chance to respond to the actual problem instead of arguing about character. The conversation may be uncomfortable, but it gives the friendship clearer information and a fairer next step. This is a practical test of “Presence Is More Than Proximity”: care becomes more reliable when expectations are spoken and behaviour can be reviewed.\n\nNotice the small decisions in this example. The people involved do not control every circumstance, but they can choose what to clarify, what to take responsibility for, and what to do next. That is often where a broad life lesson becomes useful.",
        "Friendship requires attention, reliability, respect, and freedom to be honest without humiliation. Different friendships offer different support; no single person must meet every emotional or practical need.\n\nThe deeper point is not that one response will work in every circumstance. It is that the heading gives you a question to use when the situation becomes complicated. Notice the facts, the people affected, the limits of your control, and the likely consequences of each option. A useful principle should make judgment clearer, not replace judgment altogether.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “Presence Is More Than Proximity” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
