        "Empathy is not automatic agreement. It is the effort to understand another person's situation before deciding what it means. Context can make judgment fairer without removing the need for boundaries.\n\nA common mistake is to turn a useful lesson into an absolute rule. Real situations differ: resources, responsibilities, safety, timing, and other people's choices all matter. Use the principle as a guide, then adapt it to the evidence in front of you. Thoughtful flexibility is not inconsistency; it is what makes a principle useful in real life.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “Consider the Other View” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
