        "A small project closed after the team learned that demand was lower than expected. They kept the skills and records for the next attempt.\n\nA real-life scenario: A person has spent months preparing for an opportunity, only to learn that the date has been postponed or the plan has changed. At first, the delay feels like proof that the effort was wasted. After reviewing what has already been learned, they identify which preparation remains useful, what new information is needed, and what can be done while waiting. “An Ending Is Information” does not mean pretending disappointment is pleasant. It means allowing the setback to change the plan without automatically deciding that the whole goal is impossible.\n\nNotice the small decisions in this example. The people involved do not control every circumstance, but they can choose what to clarify, what to take responsibility for, and what to do next. That is often where a broad life lesson becomes useful.",
        "An ending can contain useful evidence without defining a person's worth. Examine what happened before deciding what it says about the future.\n\nThe deeper point is not that one response will work in every circumstance. It is that the heading gives you a question to use when the situation becomes complicated. Notice the facts, the people affected, the limits of your control, and the likely consequences of each option. A useful principle should make judgment clearer, not replace judgment altogether.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “An Ending Is Information” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
