        "Begin With What Remains",
        "After a move, a student had fewer resources but still had a notebook, a schedule, and one supportive contact.\n\nA real-life scenario: Two neighbours disagree about a shared responsibility and each remembers the agreement differently. Instead of arguing from memory alone, they check the written messages, clarify what was promised, and make a new arrangement that is specific enough to avoid the same confusion. The discussion does not require either person to be perfect. It requires both to distinguish what they recall from what they can verify. This is a practical case of “Begin With What Remains”: clearer facts can make a difficult conversation more productive.\n\nNotice the small decisions in this example. The people involved do not control every circumstance, but they can choose what to clarify, what to take responsibility for, and what to do next. That is often where a broad life lesson becomes useful.",
        "A restart does not require perfect conditions. Identify available resources, limitations, and one practical first step.\n\nIn practice, this idea is less about achieving a perfect outcome than improving the quality of the process. People make wiser choices when they can explain what they know, recognise what remains uncertain, and review the result honestly. That approach leaves room for mistakes and correction without treating every setback as proof that the whole effort was pointless.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “Begin With What Remains” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
