        "A smaller experiment can provide evidence while limiting avoidable risk. Progress can be deliberate rather than dramatic.\n\nA common mistake is to turn a useful lesson into an absolute rule. Real situations differ: resources, responsibilities, safety, timing, and other people's choices all matter. Use the principle as a guide, then adapt it to the evidence in front of you. Thoughtful flexibility is not inconsistency; it is what makes a principle useful in real life.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “Make the Next Attempt Smaller” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
