        "Help can shorten confusion and reveal blind spots. Asking clearly is a skill, not proof of incapacity.\n\nConsider the consequences beyond the immediate moment. A response that feels satisfying today may create more work, damage trust, or close off a better option tomorrow. Before acting, ask what the choice will make easier, what it might make harder, and whether there is a more respectful or sustainable way to meet the same need.\n\nFor example, the question raised by “Ask for Help Without Shame” may appear in a family discussion, a classroom, a workplace, or a decision about money and time. The setting changes, but the habit of looking at evidence, context, and consequences remains valuable.",
        "Who could offer a useful perspective, and what specific question could you ask?\n\nReturn to the prompt at the end of the chapter after you have tried one action. What changed? What stayed difficult? What information did you gain? If the outcome was disappointing, separate the result from your worth and look for the next adjustment. Reflection becomes useful when it leads to a decision, a conversation, or a more informed attempt.\n\nFinal reminder for this chapter: do not wait for perfect confidence before taking a reasonable step. Start carefully, learn from the result, and adjust where needed."
